-- Passage rg6-word-parts-p02
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('337bd9ff-2f1a-578c-8da0-3ceca4af637e', 'rg6-word-parts-p02', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g6-word-parts'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Writing With Light$q$, $q$Long before phones had cameras, making a picture took patience. Early photographers in the 1800s used heavy wooden boxes and glass plates. Light entered the box and left its mark on a specially coated plate.

To make a picture of a person, a photographer asked the sitter to stay perfectly still. In the earliest years, the light needed many seconds, sometimes minutes, to mark the plate. If the person moved even a little, the picture came out blurry. Some studios used hidden metal stands to hold people's heads steady.

Over time, inventors made the process faster. New materials reacted to light more quickly, and cameras became smaller and lighter. By the early 1900s, many families could buy simple cameras and take pictures of their own birthdays and trips.

Today, historians study old photographs to learn about daily life. A single picture of a crowded street can show what people wore, how they traveled, and what stores sold. Old photographs often appear in a biography, showing a well-known person as a child.

Old photographs are fragile, so museums handle them with care. Workers wear gloves, keep the pictures away from bright light, and store them in cool, dry rooms. These steps help preserve images that are more than one hundred years old.$q$, 'draft')
